[Music]  in today's eight on your side the  pandemic has had a profound effect on  higher education  we caught up with ryan lufkin of canvas  the leading technology used by thousands  of colleges and universities in remote  learning  to get his insight on what's to come for  students and teachers post-pandemic  he says the days of the large-scale  lecture halls you know with 300-plus  students are numbered the days of those  very very large courses  in person are done i think we're going  to see a lot more  fully online courses for those very  large courses  um schools will focus on smaller courses  and things like that because uh  you know if we're going to bring people  together it should be in smaller groups  it should be in ways that they can  engage better  luvkin says we'll see a lot more of the  blended learning approach through  technology all course materials will be  online and assignments as well as  resources that students need access to  while in-person lectures and discussions  will still be available in person  he says colleges and universities have  spent the past year looking at better  ways as well to help  students get jobs we're seeing a lot  more college universities looking at  how do we provide lower cost options  lower time intensive options  beyond a two or four year degree maybe  it's a certificate program  maybe it's just a skills course how do  we offer those for  adult learners and you know maybe  students that are looking for the  fastest path of jobs  we're seeing a lot more of that that's  one of the major trends in education  right now  he adds they've been studying the  student user experience over the past  year to see how they can better design  their online courses and offer them with  greater consistency

